{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggeVsX9mKD5ZUfjcX1rL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanganate is Mn(VII), a d⁰ tetrahedral complex. Its bands cannot be analyzed with a d–d ligand-field model, so the question can't be answered reliably in that model.\n\n1. **Origin of the bands.** With no d electrons, there are no d–d transitions. The observed bands are ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) transitions, from O 2p orbitals to the empty Mn e and t₂ orbitals. The usual assignments are:\n   - 17 570 cm⁻¹: t₁ → e (¹A₂ ← ¹A₁)\n   - 31 560 cm⁻¹: t₂ → e\n   - 38 320 cm⁻¹: t₁ → t₂\n\n2. **Intensity.** The molar absorptivities are about 1 500–3 000 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. That is much too high for Laporte-forbidden d–d bands in a tetrahedral complex. It is typical of allowed charge-transfer bands.\n\n3. **Why Dq and B can't be extracted.** Dq and B come from d^n term energies, using Tanabe–Sugano matrices. A d⁰ ion has only one term, ¹S, and no d–d excited states. The three bands are charge-transfer energies, so fitting them to Dq, B and C would give meaningless numbers. The d-orbital splitting e–t₂ does show up in the differences between the LMCT bands. For example, 38 320 − 31 560 = 6 760 cm⁻¹ and 31 560 − 17 570 = 13 990 cm⁻¹ are comparable to the e–t₂ gap. But that gap is mixed with differences in ligand orbital energies and with charge-transfer electron repulsion. It is not a clean 10Dq. B is also undefined, because there are no d–d electron repulsions in the ground state.\n\nI am therefore not reporting values for Dq, B or C.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"MnO4- is d0 Mn(VII) and its bands are O→Mn charge-transfer transitions, not d-d, so Dq and B cannot be determined from the ligand-field model.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_in